.TH DEFAULT 4 "31 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
default \- the settings system commands start from
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI /etc/default/ command
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each file in
.B /etc/default
holds the settings one system command starts with, one
.IB NAME = value
a line; a line that starts with
.B #
is not in effect.
The base package ships
.BR /etc/default/login ;
the other files
.RB ( cron ,
.BR passwd ,
.BR su ,
.BR tar )
are the system's own and are described with their commands.
.SS /etc/default/login
read by
.BR login (1)
for every login:
.TP
.BI ULIMIT= blocks
the largest file a login's processes may write, in 512-byte blocks.
The base package sets
.BR 4194303 ,
the largest file the ufs file system holds (2 GiB less 512 bytes); the
system's own file leaves the line commented out, and a login is then held
to the kernel's 524288 blocks (256 MiB), so writing a bigger file fails
(EFBIG).
.TP
.BI CONSOLE= device
when set,
.B root
may log in only there; commented out (as shipped),
.B root
may log in anywhere.
.TP
.BI PASSREQ= YES\fR|\fPNO
whether every account must have a password.
.TP
.BI ALTSHELL= YES\fR|\fPNO
whether
.B SHELL
is set to the account's own shell.
.TP
.BI HZ= n
the clock rate passed on in the environment.
.TP
.BI TIMEZONE= zone
a time zone for logins; commented out, the system's
.B /etc/TIMEZONE
applies.
.TP
.B NOEXPIRE
uncommented, no password expires.
.PP
An update of the base package keeps an edited
.B /etc/default/login
and puts the new default beside it as
.BR /etc/default/login.new .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/default/login
the settings of
.BR login .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR login (1),
.BR ulimit (1)
